1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,775
A beautiful website can bring customers to a small business.

2
00:00:02,775 --> 00:00:06,291
But what happens when more orders arrive than the owner can handle?

3
00:00:08,833 --> 00:00:10,505
Other tools build you a website.

4
00:00:10,505 --> 00:00:13,976
AgentDoor prepares your business for its first difficult customer,

5
00:00:13,976 --> 00:00:16,496
starting with a rehearsal of her busiest day.

6
00:00:16,496 --> 00:00:18,388
Every refusal explained.

7
00:00:18,388 --> 00:00:20,836
Oversold: 0.

8
00:00:21,633 --> 00:00:24,199
Here's a fresh business, created live.

9
00:00:24,199 --> 00:00:27,751
A baker in Bengaluru describes her shop in Hinglish.

10
00:00:27,751 --> 00:00:31,221
Claude asks only what's missing, in her language.

11
00:00:32,033 --> 00:00:33,206
Daily capacity?

12
00:00:33,206 --> 00:00:36,631
She doesn't know yet, so it stays undecided, on purpose.

13
00:00:36,631 --> 00:00:39,810
Claude turns her words into a draft business.

14
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:43,998
Every rule keeps her own words.

15
00:00:43,998 --> 00:00:47,966
Anything she didn't say is labelled: suggested by AgentDoor.

16
00:00:49,033 --> 00:00:54,757
Rehearse tests her rules against difficult
customers and flags decisions only she can make.

17
00:00:54,757 --> 00:01:01,454
Each case shows the money at stake, with the engine's
breakdown: one thousand and eighty rupees for this rush order.

18
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:05,625
Now a simulated festival rush, before she sets a limit.

19
00:01:05,625 --> 00:01:09,723
Her undecided capacity was filled in as 10 a day: 10 of 10,

20
00:01:09,723 --> 00:01:10,906
booked.

21
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:13,134
She can really bake 6.

22
00:01:13,134 --> 00:01:16,140
She sets it, and runs a fresh simulated rush.

23
00:01:18,890 --> 00:01:20,806
Bookings stop at 6 of 6.

24
00:01:20,806 --> 00:01:24,951
The rest are refused with reasons she understands: daily limit reached,

25
00:01:24,951 --> 00:01:27,807
rush slots full, closed that day.

26
00:01:27,807 --> 00:01:31,543
Oversold: 0, against her own limit.

27
00:01:31,933 --> 00:01:36,612
Her approved products, prices and rules flow into her storefront preview.

28
00:01:36,612 --> 00:01:39,571
A preview, not a published domain.

29
00:01:42,333 --> 00:01:46,965
Meera's Kitchen, an illustrative bakery, is already live on GoDaddy.

30
00:01:46,965 --> 00:01:50,437
Its badge: rehearsed before launch, 49 checks,

31
00:01:50,437 --> 00:01:52,294
rules approved by the owner.

32
00:01:52,294 --> 00:01:57,401
Shoppers can read every rule, verified by a signed policy fingerprint.

33
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:02,438
Now a real AI customer: Claude, with the AgentDoor connector.

34
00:02:02,438 --> 00:02:05,387
It gets a signed quote: ₹800.

35
00:02:05,387 --> 00:02:08,243
I ask it to change the price to ₹500.

36
00:02:08,243 --> 00:02:09,322
It can't.

37
00:02:09,322 --> 00:02:12,933
I confirm, and the order waits for the owner, in her dashboard.

38
00:02:12,933 --> 00:02:16,054
The AI can't rewrite her prices or rules.

39
00:02:16,867 --> 00:02:20,733
Under the hood: Claude understands, AgentDoor rehearses,

40
00:02:20,733 --> 00:02:26,271
the owner approves, GoDaddy hosts, and humans and AI order.

41
00:02:26,271 --> 00:02:28,570
No model sets prices.

42
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:32,262
Quotes are signed, capacity is reserved atomically in MySQL,

43
00:02:32,262 --> 00:02:37,753
on GoDaddy Node.js hosting with an HTTPS domain and business email.

44
00:02:37,753 --> 00:02:41,362
20 orders at once: 0 oversold.

45
00:02:42,167 --> 00:02:43,688
Don't just launch a website.

46
00:02:43,688 --> 00:02:46,739
Launch a business that's ready for its customers.
